  "readout": {
    "readout_answer": "7",
    "readout_text": "To solve the problem, we begin by analyzing the total amount of orange juice and how it is distributed among the four glasses.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the Total Amount of Juice\n\nEach glass has the same capacity, and we assume the capacity of one glass is 1 unit. \n\n- The first three glasses are completely filled:  \n  $$\n  3 \\times 1 = 3 \\text{ units}\n  $$\n\n- The fourth glass is only $\\frac{1}{3}$ full:  \n  $$\n  \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ units}\n  $$\n\nSo, the total amount of juice is:\n$$\n3 + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{10}{3} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Desired Amount in Each Glass\n\nSince the total juice is $\\frac{10}{3}$ units and there are 4 glasses, each glass should have:\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3} \\div 4 = \\frac{10}{12} = \\frac{5}{6} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine How Much to Pour from Each of the First Three Glasses\n\nCurrently:\n- Each of the first three glasses has 1 unit.\n- The fourth glass has $\\frac{1}{3}$ unit.\n\nTo make all glasses have $\\frac{5}{6}$ units, we need to:\n- Remove $\\frac{1}{6}$ from each of the first three glasses (since $1 - \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{1}{6}$).\n- Add that total amount to the fourth glass.\n\nLet $x$ be the amount poured from each of the first three glasses into the fourth. Then:\n$$\n3x = \\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{5}{6} - \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n$$\nx = \\frac{1}{2} \\div 3 = \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Final Answer\n\nThe fraction of a glass that must be poured from each of the first three glasses into the fourth is:\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nThis is already in lowest terms, so $m = 1$, $n = 6$, and:\n$$\nm + n = 1 + 6 = \\boxed{7}\n$$",
